1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,126
Push an empty trolley,
and it shoots ahead.

2
00:00:03,126 --> 00:00:06,278
Push a full one just as
hard, and it barely moves.

3
00:00:06,678 --> 00:00:08,931
Kick a ball softly, then hard.

4
00:00:08,931 --> 00:00:11,030
One rule decides all of this.

5
00:00:11,480 --> 00:00:14,390
Aarav pushes his bicycle
out of the college gate.

6
00:00:14,390 --> 00:00:18,190
Then a friend sits on the carrier,
and he pushes just as hard.

7
00:00:18,540 --> 00:00:20,529
Empty, the bike leaps forward.

8
00:00:20,529 --> 00:00:22,380
With his friend, it crawls.

9
00:00:22,380 --> 00:00:23,945
Exactly how much slower?

10
00:00:24,445 --> 00:00:26,931
One rule answers that, to the decimal.

11
00:00:26,931 --> 00:00:27,983
Let's build it.

12
00:00:28,633 --> 00:00:30,991
Newton's second law links three things.

13
00:00:31,341 --> 00:00:34,983
The net force on a body, its
mass, and its acceleration.

14
00:00:35,383 --> 00:00:39,681
Net force means all the pushes and
pulls, added with their directions.

15
00:00:40,081 --> 00:00:43,847
Push a box with thirty newtons to
the right, while friction pulls back

16
00:00:43,847 --> 00:00:44,747
with ten.

17
00:00:44,544 --> 00:00:46,857
The net force is twenty newtons, right.

18
00:00:47,257 --> 00:00:50,776
Mass is how much matter
a body has, in kilograms.

19
00:00:50,776 --> 00:00:53,126
More mass is harder to speed up.

20
00:00:53,776 --> 00:00:55,302
To the motion notebook.

21
00:00:55,302 --> 00:00:58,559
A two kilogram cart on a
smooth track, no friction.

22
00:00:58,909 --> 00:01:00,665
Pull it with two newtons.

23
00:01:00,665 --> 00:01:02,509
Its velocity grows steadily.

24
00:01:02,509 --> 00:01:05,612
The acceleration is one
metre per second squared.

25
00:01:06,012 --> 00:01:08,209
Double the pull to four newtons.

26
00:01:08,209 --> 00:01:10,754
The acceleration doubles too, to two.

27
00:01:11,154 --> 00:01:12,688
Six newtons gives three.

28
00:01:12,688 --> 00:01:16,335
On the graph, the dots sit on
a straight line through zero.

29
00:01:16,335 --> 00:01:17,877
a is proportional to F.

30
00:01:18,277 --> 00:01:20,741
Now keep six newtons, and add mass.

31
00:01:20,741 --> 00:01:24,799
At four kilograms, the acceleration
halves, to one point five.

32
00:01:25,199 --> 00:01:27,111
At six kilograms, just one.

33
00:01:27,111 --> 00:01:30,448
Three times the mass, one
third of the acceleration.

34
00:01:30,848 --> 00:01:31,957
Slow-mo replay.

35
00:01:31,957 --> 00:01:34,081
Freeze, and draw the arrows.

36
00:01:34,431 --> 00:01:38,983
The orange force arrow and the red
acceleration arrow point the same way.

37
00:01:38,983 --> 00:01:41,628
The blue velocity arrow grows along them.

38
00:01:42,228 --> 00:01:43,312
One last run.

39
00:01:43,312 --> 00:01:45,381
Six newtons on the bare cart.

40
00:01:45,381 --> 00:01:47,349
Watch the moment we let go.

41
00:01:47,749 --> 00:01:48,649
Your turn.

42
00:01:48,638 --> 00:01:52,573
The moment the push stops, what
is the cart's acceleration?

43
00:01:52,573 --> 00:01:53,881
Pause and decide.

44
00:01:56,881 --> 00:01:58,211
Zero, at once!

45
00:01:58,211 --> 00:02:00,535
No net force, no acceleration.

46
00:02:00,535 --> 00:02:02,373
The cart does not stop.

47
00:02:02,873 --> 00:02:03,773
Watch.

48
00:02:03,485 --> 00:02:08,450
The force goes, a drops to zero, and
v stays at three metres per second.

49
00:02:09,100 --> 00:02:11,703
Now let's write the
rule, one step at a time.

50
00:02:12,053 --> 00:02:12,953
Step one.

51
00:02:12,834 --> 00:02:14,254
Keep the mass fixed.

52
00:02:14,254 --> 00:02:15,873
a is proportional to F.

53
00:02:16,623 --> 00:02:17,523
Step two.

54
00:02:17,406 --> 00:02:18,877
Keep the force fixed.

55
00:02:18,877 --> 00:02:21,045
a is proportional to one over m.

56
00:02:21,795 --> 00:02:22,738
Step three.

57
00:02:22,738 --> 00:02:25,639
Together, a is proportional to F over m.

58
00:02:25,639 --> 00:02:28,816
So F equals k, m, a, with k a constant.

59
00:02:29,566 --> 00:02:30,526
Step four.

60
00:02:30,526 --> 00:02:34,407
We pick the unit of force
so that k is exactly one.

61
00:02:34,407 --> 00:02:35,895
So F equals m a.

62
00:02:36,645 --> 00:02:37,604
Step five.

63
00:02:37,604 --> 00:02:43,037
One newton gives one kilogram an
acceleration of one metre per second squared.

64
00:02:43,037 --> 00:02:47,004
So one newton is one kilogram
metre per second squared.

65
00:02:47,754 --> 00:02:50,721
F is the net force, and a points along it.

66
00:02:50,721 --> 00:02:53,417
Newton first wrote this law with momentum.

67
00:02:53,417 --> 00:02:55,917
That form comes in episode fifty four.

68
00:02:56,517 --> 00:02:57,702
Remember this.

69
00:02:57,702 --> 00:03:00,648
Net force equals mass times acceleration.

70
00:03:01,348 --> 00:03:02,501
Side by side.

71
00:03:02,501 --> 00:03:04,530
The same five kilogram box.

72
00:03:04,530 --> 00:03:07,871
Ten newtons gives two
metres per second squared.

73
00:03:07,871 --> 00:03:09,291
Twenty gives four.

74
00:03:09,691 --> 00:03:12,436
Now ten newtons on a ten kilogram box.

75
00:03:12,436 --> 00:03:14,576
The acceleration drops to one.

76
00:03:14,576 --> 00:03:16,705
More mass, less acceleration.

77
00:03:17,305 --> 00:03:18,307
Now you try.

78
00:03:18,307 --> 00:03:21,158
Double the force, and double the mass too.

79
00:03:21,158 --> 00:03:23,298
What happens to the acceleration?

80
00:03:23,298 --> 00:03:24,543
Pause and decide.

81
00:03:27,543 --> 00:03:29,015
It stays the same!

82
00:03:29,015 --> 00:03:30,338
a is F over m.

83
00:03:30,338 --> 00:03:34,159
Twice the force over twice
the mass is the same ratio.

84
00:03:34,809 --> 00:03:36,544
Now, one worked example.

85
00:03:36,895 --> 00:03:40,250
Aarav and his bike have a
mass of eighty kilograms.

86
00:03:40,250 --> 00:03:45,175
He pedals with one sixty newtons, and
friction and air push back with forty.

87
00:03:45,175 --> 00:03:48,817
Find his acceleration, and
his speed after four seconds.

88
00:03:49,317 --> 00:03:50,954
First, the net force.

89
00:03:50,954 --> 00:03:53,460
One sixty forward, minus forty back.

90
00:03:53,460 --> 00:03:56,166
One hundred and twenty newtons, forward.

91
00:03:56,916 --> 00:03:58,874
Then a equals F over m.

92
00:03:58,874 --> 00:04:04,083
One twenty divided by eighty is one
point five metres per second squared.

93
00:04:04,833 --> 00:04:06,870
From rest, v equals a t.

94
00:04:06,870 --> 00:04:10,496
One point five times four
is six metres per second.

95
00:04:11,246 --> 00:04:12,290
Now the hook.

96
00:04:12,290 --> 00:04:15,914
With his seventy kilogram
friend, the mass is one fifty.

97
00:04:15,914 --> 00:04:18,948
The same net force gives
only zero point eight.

98
00:04:19,598 --> 00:04:21,063
Now, the exam trap.

99
00:04:21,413 --> 00:04:25,139
Many students put the push
alone into F equals m a.

100
00:04:25,139 --> 00:04:28,520
Fifty newtons on a ten
kilogram box gives five.

101
00:04:28,920 --> 00:04:31,299
But friction pulls back with twenty.

102
00:04:31,299 --> 00:04:33,550
F in this law is the net force.

103
00:04:33,550 --> 00:04:36,481
Thirty newtons over ten
kilograms gives three.

104
00:04:37,131 --> 00:04:38,555
Where is this asked?

105
00:04:38,555 --> 00:04:42,924
In questions that find acceleration
from the net force, on blocks, ropes

106
00:04:42,924 --> 00:04:43,824
and lifts.

107
00:04:44,355 --> 00:04:45,633
Exam level now.

108
00:04:45,633 --> 00:04:50,039
A constant force acts on a two
kilogram body for four seconds.

109
00:04:50,039 --> 00:04:55,019
Its velocity goes from four i plus
three j, to minus four i minus three

110
00:04:55,019 --> 00:04:55,919
j.

111
00:04:55,463 --> 00:04:57,585
Find the size of the force.

112
00:04:57,585 --> 00:04:58,916
Pause and solve.

113
00:05:04,316 --> 00:05:05,764
The answer is C.

114
00:05:05,764 --> 00:05:06,909
Five newtons.

115
00:05:07,409 --> 00:05:11,263
The change in velocity is
minus eight i, minus six j.

116
00:05:11,263 --> 00:05:13,759
Its size is ten metres per second.

117
00:05:14,259 --> 00:05:18,742
So the acceleration is ten over four,
two point five metres per second

118
00:05:18,742 --> 00:05:19,642
squared.

119
00:05:19,961 --> 00:05:23,824
F equals m a, two times two
point five, five newtons.

120
00:05:23,824 --> 00:05:27,195
The speed stayed five,
but the velocity reversed.

121
00:05:27,795 --> 00:05:29,255
Here is your recap.

122
00:05:29,255 --> 00:05:30,757
Screenshot this card.

123
00:05:33,957 --> 00:05:35,888
Can you answer these without help?

124
00:05:36,288 --> 00:05:37,188
One.

125
00:05:36,774 --> 00:05:39,979
The same push on an empty
bike and a loaded bike.

126
00:05:39,979 --> 00:05:42,087
Which speeds up more, and why?

127
00:05:42,987 --> 00:05:43,887
Two.

128
00:05:43,493 --> 00:05:45,942
What is one newton, in base units?

129
00:05:46,842 --> 00:05:47,742
Three.

130
00:05:47,496 --> 00:05:50,899
Why must F in F equals
m a be the net force?

131
00:05:51,799 --> 00:05:56,304
Next time: Aarav pushes his study
table, and he rolls backwards.

132
00:05:56,304 --> 00:05:57,537
Who pushed him?

133
00:05:57,537 --> 00:05:58,927
Newton's third law.
